# 5. Wazuh Detection Engineering

## 5.1 Windows Authentication Monitoring

Windows Security Event ID `4625` represents a failed logon attempt.

The project uses this event to identify unsuccessful authentication attempts.

A single failed authentication can be generated by an incorrect password or an invalid account.

However, multiple failed authentication attempts against the same account within a short period may indicate brute-force activity.

## 5.2 Custom Brute-Force Detection Rule

A custom Wazuh rule was created to detect repeated failed authentication attempts.

| Detection Property | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Rule ID | 100100 |
| Severity Level | 10 |
| Detection Condition | Three or more failed authentication events for the same username within 60 seconds |
| MITRE ATT&CK | T1110 – Brute Force |

The rule uses Wazuh rule `60122` as its parent detection condition.

### Custom Rule Configuration

```xml
<group name="local,windows,authentication_failed,">

  <rule id="100100" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="60">

    <if_matched_sid>60122</if_matched_sid>

    <same_field>win.eventdata.targetUserName</same_field>

    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>

    <description>
      Possible brute-force attack: 3 or more failed
      Windows logins for the same account within 60 seconds.
    </description>

    <group>authentication_failed,brute_force,windows,</group>

  </rule>

</group>
```

**Screenshot 05: Custom Wazuh Rule Configuration**

<!-- Add screenshot: docs/images/05-custom-wazuh-rule.png -->

### Rule Explanation

- `id="100100"`: Identifies the custom detection rule.
- `level="10"`: Assigns a high severity level.
- `frequency="3"`: Requires three matching events.
- `timeframe="60"`: Defines the 60-second detection window.
- `if_matched_sid`: Uses the existing failed-logon detection rule.
- `same_field`: Ensures the failed logons relate to the same username.
- `T1110`: Maps the detection to the MITRE ATT&CK Brute Force technique.

## 5.3 Brute-Force Simulation

A controlled test was conducted on the Windows endpoint.

The following command was used:

```cmd
runas /user:TARGET-PC\fakeuser cmd
```

An incorrect password was entered repeatedly within the configured detection window.

The Wazuh Manager successfully detected the activity and generated alerts under custom Rule ID `100100`.

The resulting alerts included:

- Agent: `TARGET-PC`
- Agent ID: `001`
- Rule ID: `100100`
- Severity: `10`
- Description: Possible brute-force attack.
- MITRE ATT&CK: `T1110`

Six matching alerts were subsequently retrieved from the Wazuh Indexer during the Python collector test.

This demonstrated that the custom detection rule was operational.

